# SSH keys
*.pem
*.pub

# Reference PDFs (course material etc.) — not part of the codebase
*.pdf

# Terraform: your own settings and all generated state.
# Do NOT add a blanket `terraform/` here — that is the live stack and must stay tracked. Only the
# generated/secret files below are ignored. (.terraform/ holds ~800MB of provider binaries; a blanket
# rule was briefly in place while terraform/ was a dead leftover, and it hid the whole stack.)
# NOTE: terraform.tfstate* (glob) also covers the timestamped backups Terraform writes
# (terraform.tfstate.1784477786.backup), which a bare ".backup" pattern misses.
terraform/voteball.tfvars
terraform/terraform.tfstate*
terraform/.terraform.tfstate.lock.info
terraform/tfplan
terraform/.terraform/
# Written by scripts/find-latest-snapshot.sh before each apply; machine-specific, never committed.
terraform/snapshot.auto.tfvars
# Written by scripts/bootstrap-tf-backend.sh. Carries the state bucket name, which embeds the AWS
# account id -- a backend block cannot interpolate variables, so this is the only way to keep
# account identity out of a committed file. backend.hcl.example is the committed template.
terraform/backend.hcl

# Deploy-time credentials read by scripts/deploy.sh (admin + Jenkins usernames and passwords).
# Same category as terraform/voteball.tfvars above: your own settings, never committed. Keeping it in
# the repo folder rather than $HOME means it sits next to the tfvars it pairs with -- but it holds
# PLAINTEXT passwords, so it is ignored here and must stay that way.
deploy.env

# (The Jenkins build host used to be a separate Terraform stack with its own state and its own
# ignore rules. It moved into the EKS cluster on 2026-07-30/31 and terraform/jenkins/ no longer
# exists, so those rules were removed -- the terraform/ rules above are all that is needed.)

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.pytest_cache/
.venv/

# Local agent/session state — never belongs in the repo.
.superpowers/
.remember/
.claude/settings.local.json

# Personal notes (not part of the submission)
EXPLAINER.md
PROJECT-QA.md

# Teacher's review documents. Received 2026-08-23 as .docx for Task 3 (Kubernetes/Docker) and Task 4
# (Jenkins CI/CD); their findings were applied to the repo and are recorded in
# docs/design/2026-08-23-release-branch-and-digest-design.md and the commits around it. The documents
# themselves are correspondence, not submission artifacts -- and a binary blob no test or grep can
# read is the worst possible place for a claim about this repo to live.
*.docx

# Written by Jenkinsfile-cd's Input Validation stage (scripts/ci/resolve-digests.sh) and read by
# Promote. It lives in the agent workspace, which is a git checkout -- so on 2026-08-23 a `git add -A`
# in promote-to-release.sh committed it onto the `release` branch, and the NEXT promotion then failed
# its checkout because the file existed on both sides. The add is now scoped; this is the second lock.
image-digests.tsv
